## Fraud Detection 
 Bank and card fraud is a common problem that loses both banks and people money and trust. DSML can be used to help solve this problem via machine learning models trained on the typical behaviors of a client.

## Technical explanation 
A machine learning model trained using multiple client features such as time of purchase, location of purchase, amount spent, and whether or not it was from an unusual merchant could be used in conjunction with a boolean fraudulent label. For example, if most purchases are made in winnipeg, under 500$, then a 3000 dollar purchase in toronto would be labelled fraudulent with a high degree of certainty due to the unusual amount, location and merchant. The best training algorithm for this, in my opinion, would be logistic regression, as it is used to predict the probability of something being true or false. Using this, we can also sort output into multiple labels, such as definite fraud, likely fraud, or unlikely fraud, depending on where the model lands between true and false for a fraudulent purchase.

## Business explanation 
Bank and card fraud poses a lot of risks for both the bank and the client. In most cases of card fraud, you need to expend time and resources in filing the appropriate paperwork, recovering stolen money and gathering the information you need to solve the case. Not only this, but your clients will become much more hesitant in using their cards for online shopping, and they may even lose trust in the institution. Our solution is to detect and stop fraudulent purchases as they occur. You can expect a major cutdown on fraud reports and substantially less funds going towards the recovery and stoppage of fraud. The best part is that all of this occurs in the background, so your team can continue to work effectively without being bogged down by dealing with fraud.

 

